= The Constitution of 1931

The Constituent Cortes has written the most democratic constitution in Spanish history: universal suffrage including women, a single-chamber parliament, regional autonomy, no established religion. But two articles cut deepest. **Article 26** strips the Church of state funding, bars the religious orders from teaching, and dissolves the Jesuits - a settling of accounts that the Catholic world will never forgive. **Article 44** allows the nationalization of property, written in with <span style="color: #c00000;">**PSOE**</span> votes over the objections of the moderate republicans.

Niceto Alcalá-Zamora, the Catholic president of the Council, resigns rather than sign the religious articles, and Azaña takes his place as Prime Minister - though Alcalá-Zamora will return as President of the Republic. We have written our convictions into the Republic's founding law.

[? if in_republican_socialist : Our republican partners swallowed Article 44 for the sake of the coalition; they will not forget that they did. ?] The cost is a Catholic Spain that now regards the Republic as an enemy of the faith - and a reservoir of grievance the <span style="color: #0087DC;">**CEDA**</span> will spend the next four years filling.
